{
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "lights",
   "title": "The town in festival lights",
   "note": "Eve of the rathotsava · 23 November",
   "text": "On the eve of the chariot festival the streets around the temple fill with stalls, arches of light and a giant wheel."
  },
  {
   "id": "caturdashi",
   "title": "Caturdaśī night at the temple",
   "note": "23 November",
   "text": "The night before the full moon is Caturdaśī. For this night the temple's order of worship gives the mahā-raṅga-pūje and the siṃha-vāhanotsava, the procession on the lion vehicle, with the savāri to the eastern kaṭṭe. The utsava-mūrti goes out among the people under parasols and by torchlight."
  },
  {
   "id": "morning",
   "title": "Morning of the Koḍi Habba",
   "note": "Full moon of Vṛścika · 24 November",
   "text": "The rathotsava falls on the full moon of the lunar month of Kārttika or Mārgaśira that falls in the solar month of Vṛścika; the sthala-purāṇa says that on this day Īśvara appeared as a crore of liṅgas. North of the temple lies the Koṭitīrtha, where, the purāṇa says, every act yields its fruit a crore-fold. Sugarcane, which also adorns the chariot, is sold all along the street."
  },
  {
   "id": "rathorohana",
   "title": "Rathārohaṇa",
   "note": "The ascent of the chariot",
   "text": "After the special mahā-pūje, at an auspicious hour of the morning, the deity ascends the chariot: “the members of the Aithal family carry the silver mūrti to the chariot.” The assembled devotees then draw the Brahmaratha along the chariot street, and people say that at this moment the bird Garuḍa appears in the sky."
  },
  {
   "id": "brahmaratha",
   "title": "The Brahmaratha",
   "note": "Six wheels, forty-two feet to the kalaśa",
   "text": "The chariot is a six-wheeled syandana, with wheels eight feet across. Its fixed part, from the base to the maṇṭapa, stands about eighteen feet; with the cloth gopura and the kalaśa it rises to about forty-two. The beams of the base end in elephants and crocodiles, and its panels carry deities, mithuna figures, warriors and rows of swans. It may date from the Keḷadi period, and resembles the Brahmaratha of Kukke Subrahmaṇya."
  },
  {
   "id": "santarpane",
   "title": "Mahā-santarpaṇe and the temple",
   "note": "Midday and afternoon · 24 November",
   "text": "At midday the mahā-santarpaṇe, the great feeding, is held and prasāda is shared. In the evening the deity descends from the chariot and, with the aṣṭāvadhāna-sēve, returns to the temple."
  }
 ],
